5.2. Semantic segmentation on S2. After having reviewed equivariant nonlinearities, we
can now proceed to discuss concrete equivariant network architectures. For computer vision
tasks such as semantic segmentation and object detection, the standard flat image space of Z2
is a homogeneous space under translation and hence falls in the class of convolutions discussed
in Section 3. The standard convolution (1.3) is, by (3.10), the natural Z2 equivariant layer in
this context. Let’s now take the first concrete steps into a more complex example of how the
equivariant structure on homogeneous spaces can be applied in a more interesting setup.
Moving to the sphere S2 provides a simple example of a non-trivial homogeneous space as
the input manifold. As detailed in (1.2), a semantic segmentation model can now be viewed
as a map
N : L2(S2, R3) → L2(S2, P (Ω)) , (5.13)
where P (Ω) is the space of probability distributions over the N classes Ω. The output features
transform as scalars under the group action on the input manifold. In the notation of (4.3)
and (4.4), this means that X = Y = S2, V1 = R3 and V2 = RN. The symmetry group should
act on the output space in the same way as on the input space, i.e. σ2 = σ1. Since the output
class labels do not carry any directional information, we have ρ2 = idN.
Viewed as a quotient space S2 = SO(3)/SO(2), the sphere is a homogeneous space where
each point can be associated with a particular rotation. One possible parametrization would
be in terms of latitude (θ) and longitude (φ), i.e. the latitude specifies the angular distance
from the north pole whereas the longitude specifies the angular distance from a meridian.
The corresponding convolution following from (3.13) can be formulated as an integral over
S2 using this parametrization. In practice there are much more efficient formulations using
the spectral structure on the sphere, see Section 6 for a treatment of how convolutions can be
performed using a Fourier decomposition.
