6.4. Output features in SE(3). As an example of a possible application of the techniques
presented in this section, consider the problem of 3D object detection in pictures taken by
fisheye cameras. These cameras have a spherical image plane and therefore, the components
of the input feature map f transform as scalars under the regular representation (cf. (4.3)):
[π1(R)f ](x) = f (R−1x) . (6.28)
Since this is the transformation property considered in the context of spherical CNNs, the
entire network can be built using the layers discussed in this section.
As detailed in Section 5.3, for object detection, we want to identify the class, physical size,
position and orientation for each object in the image. This can be realized by associating to
each pixel in the output picture (which is often of lower resolution than the input) a class
probability vector p ∈ P (Ω), a size vector s ∈ R3 containing height, width and depth of the
object, a position vector x ∈ R3 and an orientation given by a matrix Q ∈ SO(3). Here, P (Ω)
is the space of probability distributions in N classes as in (1.2). In the framework outlined
above, this means that the output feature map takes values in P (Ω) ⊕ R3 ⊕ R3 ⊕ SO(3).
If the fisheye camera rotates by R ∈ SO(3), the output features have to transform
accordingly. In particular, since the classification and the size of the object do not depend on
the rotation, p transforms as N scalars and s transforms as three scalars:
ρ2(R)p = p, ρ2(R)s = s , (6.29)
where we used the notation introduced in (4.4). The position vector x on the other hand
transforms in the fundamental representation of SO(3)
ρ2(R)x = R · x . (6.30)
Finally, the rotation matrix Q transforms by a similarity transformation
ρ2(R)Q = R · Q · RT . (6.31)
